    {"id": "v-l-arpege", "cat": "berth", "tier": "berth_top", "priority": 1, "badge": "3 MICHELIN STARS", "name": "L'Arpège", "short": "L'Arpège", "lat": 48.8557, "lng": 2.3186, "tags": ["7th · Faubourg Saint-Germain", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Haute cuisine végétale"], "why": "Passard took over the address from his old boss Alain Senderens, whose L'Archestrate had stood there, and renamed it L'Arpège. In 2001 he dropped red meat; in July 2025 he dropped every animal product except his own honey, and the 2026 MICHELIN guide still lists the house among the nine three-star tables in Paris. What arrives comes from his three kitchen gardens, picked daily and driven in, so the menu shifts with the week rather than the season.", "address": "84 rue de Varenne, 75007 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=L%27Arp%C3%A8ge+Paris", "category": "creme", "verdict": "One of the most influential kitchens in modern Paris, and you can trace the bloodline: alumni run Septime (Bertrand Grébaut trained here) and Le Servan (Tatiana Levha, ex-Arpège, ex-Astrance). In July 2025 Passard went further than anyone at his level, striking meat, fish, shellfish, dairy and eggs from the menu — the first three-star in the world to do it, with only honey from his own hives surviving. Come for the vegetables off his own gardens, not for value, and not for the egg you've read about.", "signature": "The house was built on the chaud-froid d'oeuf, the \"Arpège egg\": a warm farm-egg yolk under cold four-spice cream sharpened with sherry vinegar and chives, a thread of maple, served in its own shell. Verify before you count on it — the July 2025 plant-based turn stripped eggs, cream and butter, so what reaches the table now is the garden: vegetables, fruit, herbs and flowers from Passard's own plots in the Sarthe and by Mont-Saint-Michel.", "caveat": "Not a value eat, and the prices quoted in 2026 sources disagree widely, so ask when you book. Since July 2025 the animal cooking that made Passard's name, the lobster and the pigeon, is gone, and so is the famous egg: the kitchen is all-vegetable but for the honey from its own hives. Portions can feel small for the money, and a table is hard-won.", "person": "Alain Passard", "signal_chip": {"label": "3 MICHELIN Stars", "full": "Three MICHELIN Stars, Guide MICHELIN France 2026", "cosign": "MICHELIN"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "Alain Passard's three-star on rue de Varenne, all-vegetable since July 2025 but for honey from his hives.", "dishes": [{"name": "Tomato mosaic", "note": "A summer plate of sliced garden tomatoes, named in reporting on the all-plant menu", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Flambéed aubergine with melon confit", "note": "Aubergine flamed at the pass, sweetened with a melon preserve; cited from the plant-based menu", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Onion, carrot, shallot and cabbage", "note": "An assortment built from the alliums and roots of Passard's own plots", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Garden crudités", "note": "Raw vegetables from the three gardens, served early in the tasting", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Mon-Fri 12:30-14:30 and 19:30-22:30, closed Sat-Sun (Mes Prestiges, May 2026; not checked on the own site)", "price_range": "Call ahead: Gault&Millau 2026 gives EUR 260-420 per person before drinks, another Gault&Millau page EUR 185-520; Mes Prestiges gives lunch from EUR 175 and dinner from EUR 490 (seen Oct 2026)", "reservation": "Book well ahead; lunch is the easier seating to get", "neighborhood": "7th · Faubourg Saint-Germain", "charter": {"price": "Call ahead: Gault&Millau 2026 gives EUR 260-420 per person before drinks, another Gault&Millau page EUR 185-520; Mes Prestiges gives lunch from EUR 175 and dinner from EUR 490 (seen Oct 2026)", "book": "Book well ahead; lunch is the easier seating to get", "warn": "Not a value eat, and the prices quoted in 2026 sources disagree widely, so ask when you book. Since July 2025 the animal cooking that made Passard's name, the lobster and the pigeon, is gone, and so is the famous egg: the kitchen is all-vegetable but for the honey from its own hives. Portions can feel small for the money, and a table is hard-won.", "fit": "Alain Passard's three-star on rue de Varenne, all-vegetable since July 2025 but for honey from his hives."}},
    {"id": "v-plenitude-cheval-blanc-paris", "cat": "shop", "tier": "several", "priority": 2, "badge": "3 MICHELIN STARS", "name": "Plénitude - Cheval Blanc Paris", "short": "Plénitude - Cheval Blanc Paris", "lat": 48.8586, "lng": 2.342, "tags": ["1st · Pont-Neuf", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Haute cuisine - 3-star tasting"], "why": "The room opened inside Cheval Blanc, the hotel cut out of the old Samaritaine store, and about six months later, in March 2022, MICHELIN gave it all three stars at once. Donckele numbers his sauces like perfumes and builds each menu backwards from them: the langoustine or turbot arrives as the thing that carries a broth, a vinaigrette or a jus. The 2026 guide kept the three stars, and a June 2026 review on Trois Etoiles shows the menus still running.", "address": "Cheval Blanc Paris, 8 Quai du Louvre, 75001 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Pl%C3%A9nitude+-+Cheval+Blanc+Paris+Paris", "category": "creme", "verdict": "Donckele's second three-star kitchen, and one that lived up to its billing rather than coasting on the Cheval Blanc name: three MICHELIN stars in barely seven months - awarded at the March 2022 Cognac ceremony, straight from zero to three - and held every year since, through the 2026 guide, backed by Gault&Millau 19/20 (five toques) and No. 14 on the World's 50 Best 2025. Here the sauce, not the protein, is the dish.", "signature": "The sauces themselves. Donckele works as a self-styled saucier-parfumeur, numbering his layered sauces like perfumes into what he calls his \"Absolues,\" and the two carte-blanche menus (Fuguons ensemble in 4 acts, Symphonie in 6) are built so that langoustine, turbot or pigeon serve as condiments to their broths, vinaigrettes and jus - named plates like La Langoustine Sabayon Cardinal Thenors and Turbot Tellurienne d'Hiver, reported by Fine Dining Lovers. Pastry by Maxime Frédéric, named World's Best Pastry Chef 2025.", "caveat": "Not a discovery, a summit - and priced like one. The six-act Symphonie ran EUR 480 in 2025 (the four-act Fuguons ensemble EUR 445), with wine pairings up to EUR 495 on top; dinner only, Tuesday to Saturday, in a hushed 26-seat room above the Seine. The sauce-as-hero concept is cerebral and formal by design - go for the thrill of watching a master saucier work, not for comfort food or a casual night.", "person": "Arnaud Donckele", "signal_chip": {"label": "3 Stars · 2026", "full": "Three MICHELIN stars since March 2022, kept in the Guide MICHELIN France 2026; No. 14 on The World's 50 Best Restaurants 2025 (the 2026 list is announced 4 November 2026 in Lima).", "cosign": "MICHELIN Guide France 2026; The World's 50 Best Restaurants 2025"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "Arnaud Donckele's sauce-first tasting room over the Seine at Cheval Blanc, three stars since 2022.", "dishes": [{"name": "Langoustine, sabayon Cardinal Thenors", "note": "A named plate where the sabayon, not the shellfish, is the point", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Turbot Tellurienne d'Hiver", "note": "Winter turbot as a carrier for one of Donckele's layered sauces", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Symphonie", "note": "The six-act carte-blanche menu built around the sauces", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Maxime Frédéric's desserts", "note": "Pastry by the chef named World's Best Pastry Chef 2025", "kind": "dish"}], "neighborhood": "1st · Pont-Neuf", "charter": {"warn": "Not a discovery, a summit - and priced like one. The six-act Symphonie ran EUR 480 in 2025 (the four-act Fuguons ensemble EUR 445), with wine pairings up to EUR 495 on top; dinner only, Tuesday to Saturday, in a hushed 26-seat room above the Seine. The sauce-as-hero concept is cerebral and formal by design - go for the thrill of watching a master saucier work, not for comfort food or a casual night.", "fit": "Arnaud Donckele's sauce-first tasting room over the Seine at Cheval Blanc, three stars since 2022."}},
    {"id": "v-kei", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 4, "badge": "MICHELIN 3 STARS", "name": "Kei", "short": "Kei", "lat": 48.8646, "lng": 2.3428, "tags": ["1st · Louvre", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Haute cuisine (Franco-Japanese)"], "why": "Kobayashi opened here in 2011 after years under Gilles Goujon and Alain Ducasse, and in 2020 became the first Japanese-born chef to hold three MICHELIN stars in France; the 2026 guide kept them. His father cooked kaiseki in Nagano, and the editing shows: a January 2026 Yonder review describes langoustine smoked over binchotan and pigeon lacquered with red miso. Gault&Millau's 2026 page scores it 17/20.", "address": "5 rue Coq-Héron, 75001 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Kei+Paris", "category": "creme", "verdict": "France's only Japanese-born three-star chef, and the room has held all three MICHELIN stars every year since 2020 — this is precision plated as spectacle, not comfort or improvisation.", "signature": "Le jardin de légumes croquants — a \"garden\" of up to forty seasonal ingredients, crisp raw vegetables and herbs layered over Scottish smoked salmon, bound with a rocket cream and tomato vinaigrette and crowned with a cloud of lemon foam and a black-olive-and-almond crumble. It took five years to perfect and shifts with the season, so no two plates are quite alike.", "caveat": "Ceremony and expense, not a spontaneous dinner: 2026 sources give menus from about EUR 185 up to EUR 580 and disagree on which runs at lunch, so confirm when you book. The room is small and books out.", "person": "Kei Kobayashi — chef-owner. Born in Nagano, Japan, the son of a traditional kaiseki cook; he turned to French cooking after watching a documentary about French cuisine, moved to France in 1998, and trained under Gilles Goujon (Auberge du Vieux Puits) and Alain Ducasse before opening Kei in 2011.", "signal_chip": {"label": "MICHELIN 3 stars", "full": "Three MICHELIN stars since 2020, retained in the 2026 Guide — Kei Kobayashi was the first Japanese chef to earn three stars in France.", "cosign": "MICHELIN Guide 2026"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "Kei Kobayashi's small three-star off rue Coq-Héron: French produce, Japanese precision, by the Louvre.", "dishes": [{"name": "Jardin de légumes croquants", "note": "Up to forty raw vegetables and herbs over smoked salmon, rocket cream and lemon foam; five years in the making", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Langoustine smoked over binchotan", "note": "Named in Yonder's January 2026 review", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Pigeon lacquered with red miso", "note": "The meat course Yonder singled out in January 2026", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Tue-Sat, lunch 12:30-14:00, dinner 19:30-21:00 (2026 listing extract; not checked on the own site)", "price_range": "Call ahead: Gault&Millau 2026 gives EUR 195-580 per person before drinks; Yonder (Jan 2026) menus from EUR 185; Mes Prestiges lunch about EUR 140, dinner EUR 310-380 (seen Oct 2026)", "reservation": "Online; bookings open on the first Tuesday of each month, two months ahead (old record), and fill fast", "neighborhood": "1st · Louvre", "charter": {"price": "Call ahead: Gault&Millau 2026 gives EUR 195-580 per person before drinks; Yonder (Jan 2026) menus from EUR 185; Mes Prestiges lunch about EUR 140, dinner EUR 310-380 (seen Oct 2026)", "book": "Online; bookings open on the first Tuesday of each month, two months ahead (old record), and fill fast", "warn": "Ceremony and expense, not a spontaneous dinner: 2026 sources give menus from about EUR 185 up to EUR 580 and disagree on which runs at lunch, so confirm when you book. The room is small and books out.", "fit": "Kei Kobayashi's small three-star off rue Coq-Héron: French produce, Japanese precision, by the Louvre."}},
    {"id": "v-table-by-bruno-verjus", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 5, "badge": "CLOSES 22 DEC 2026", "name": "Table by Bruno Verjus", "short": "Table by Bruno Verjus", "lat": 48.8497, "lng": 2.3766, "tags": ["12th · Aligre", "Not confirmed for 2026 — call ahead"], "productTags": ["Produce-driven haute cuisine (counter tasting)"], "why": "Verjus announced in an Instagram video that Table will serve for the last time on 22 December 2026, thirteen years after it opened in April 2013. His reason, quoted by Gault&Millau, is that 'fine dining is dead': he no longer wants a cooking whose price shuts most people out, and he plans to write, in Greece. Until then it keeps its two MICHELIN stars and its single daily Couleur du jour menu, which put it at No. 3 in the world in 2024.", "address": "3 rue de Prague, 75012 Paris, France", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Table+by+Bruno+Verjus+Paris", "category": "produce", "verdict": "The two MICHELIN stars and the No. 8-in-the-world ranking are earned, not hype: one daily seating at a wave-shaped counter, Verjus plating in front of you, ingredient-worship over technique for its own sake. Book it and you'll watch a self-taught ex-food-critic cook the day's tide and fields into a single 'Couleur du jour' tasting.", "signature": "Lobster 'mi-cru mi-cuit' — half-raw, half-cooked, trap-caught off the Ile d'Yeu, the shells toasted and the flesh warmed in ghee to roughly the temperature of the mouth — the dish critics reach for first when they describe him. The meal ends, as it always does, on his chocolate-and-caper tart under a spoon of caviar.", "caveat": "Closing for good: the last service is 22 December 2026. One fixed tasting, no a la carte, at a counter of about two dozen seats, EUR 480 before drinks; expect the final weeks to be the hardest tables in Paris to land.", "person": "Bruno Verjus, chef-patron, in his mid-sixties. Self-taught cook with a scattered pedigree before the stove: former medical student, entrepreneur in China, photographer, author and radio food critic. He came to the kitchen only in his mid-fifties, opened Table in 2013, and took it to two MICHELIN stars and the World's 50 Best top ten.", "signal_chip": {"label": "Closes 22 Dec", "full": "Bruno Verjus has announced Table's final service for 22 December 2026. It still holds two MICHELIN stars and was No. 8 on The World's 50 Best Restaurants 2025 (No. 3 in 2024).", "cosign": "Gault&Millau; Sortiraparis (Sept 2026)"}, "status": "time_limited", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "Bruno Verjus's two-star counter by the Marché d'Aligre, closing for good after service on 22 December 2026.", "dishes": [{"name": "Lobster mi-cru mi-cuit", "note": "Trap-caught off the Ile d'Yeu, warmed in ghee to about mouth temperature; his most described plate", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Chocolate and caper tart with caviar", "note": "The closing plate, a spoon of caviar on a bitter chocolate tart", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Couleur du jour", "note": "The one daily tasting, written from that morning's market and catch", "kind": "dish"}], "price_range": "Couleur du jour menu EUR 480 before drinks (Sortiraparis, updated 9 Sept 2026; seen Oct 2026)", "reservation": "Book now if at all: one set seating, non-refundable deposit at booking, last service 22 December 2026", "neighborhood": "12th · Aligre"},
    {"id": "v-datil", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 7, "badge": "1★ + GREEN STAR", "name": "Datil", "short": "Datil", "lat": 48.8646, "lng": 2.3535, "tags": ["3rd · Arts et Métiers", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Plant-forward gastronomy"], "why": "Fleury named the place after the date palm and opened it in September 2023, her first restaurant of her own after kitchens from L'Astrance to Blue Hill at Stone Barns. In March 2026 MICHELIN gave Datil a Green Star, one of only two new ones in Paris that year (the other went to La Borie). The menu opens on a broth made from vegetable peelings and ends with a tisane brewed from plum stones.", "address": "13 rue des Gravilliers, 75003 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Datil+Paris", "category": "produce", "verdict": "One of Paris's clearest proofs that a near-meatless tasting menu can carry a MICHELIN star — and, since 2026, a Green Star — without a single luxury crutch: no caviar, no wagyu, no truffle-for-show, animal protein used only as a condiment.", "signature": "The zero-waste tasting menu: it opens with a bouillon drawn from vegetable peelings (potato peel, at last report) and closes with a tisane brewed from plum stones — a nod to the restaurant's name. Plants lead; meat plays a background extra.", "caveat": "Tasting menu only, and the prices have climbed: 2026 sources range from about EUR 90 at lunch to EUR 160 at dinner (seen Oct 2026), so confirm when you book.", "person": "Manon Fleury — chef-owner, a former French junior sabre-fencing champion who trained at Ferrandi and cooked at L'Astrance (under Pascal Barbot) and Blue Hill at Stone Barns (under Dan Barber), then ran the kitchens at Le Mermoz, Elsa at the Monte-Carlo Beach and Le Perchoir before opening Datil in September 2023. Co-chef Laurene Barjhoux (ex-Arpege / Alain Passard) runs the hierarchy-free, women-led brigade beside her.", "signal_chip": {"label": "1★ + Green Star", "full": "1 MICHELIN Star (2024) and MICHELIN Green Star (2026)", "cosign": "MICHELIN Guide"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "Manon Fleury's plant-led tasting room in the Marais, one MICHELIN star and a 2026 Green Star.", "dishes": [{"name": "Peelings bouillon", "note": "The opening broth drawn from vegetable peel (potato peel at last report)", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Plum-stone tisane", "note": "The closing infusion, brewed from fruit stones", "kind": "drink"}, {"name": "Seven-course dinner tasting", "note": "Plants at the centre, meat or fish used as seasoning", "kind": "dish"}], "price_range": "Call ahead: lunch EUR 90-95, dinner about EUR 140; Gault&Millau gives EUR 95-160 before drinks (seen Oct 2026)", "reservation": "Book ahead; small room, tasting menu only", "neighborhood": "3rd · Arts et Métiers"},
    {"id": "v-le-rigmarole", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 9, "badge": "EX-MICHELIN STAR", "name": "Le Rigmarole", "short": "Le Rigmarole", "lat": 48.8646, "lng": 2.369, "tags": ["11th · Oberkampf", "Not confirmed for 2026 — call ahead"], "productTags": ["Binchotan yakitori x handmade Italian pasta"], "why": "Robert Compagnon and Jessica Yang met in Guy Savoy's kitchen and opened this room on rue du Grand Prieuré, grilling over Japanese binchotan under 1920s Tiffany lamps while the pasta is rolled by hand behind the counter. Their yakitori thigh is brushed with clementine zest; the oyster goes on the grill with herbs and garlic. Next door the pair also run Folderol, which Time Out called the first combined ice-cream shop and natural-wine shop in Paris.", "address": "10 rue du Grand Prieuré, 75011 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Le+Rigmarole+Paris", "category": "produce", "verdict": "A walnut counter where Robert Compagnon grills yakitori over Japanese binchotan while Jessica Yang's kitchen rolls the pasta by hand. It held a MICHELIN star from 2020 until the 2023 guide; the spaghetti with bottarga is still the plate regulars come back for, and the counter seats are few.", "signature": "Spaghetti with bottarga: house-made pasta under a snow of grated cured mullet roe. Its charcoal-side twin is the yakitori \"thigh,\" perfumed with clementine zest.", "caveat": "Small counter plus a few tables, tasting-menu driven and not cheap. Its MICHELIN star (awarded 2020) was withdrawn in the 2023 guide per Gilles Pudlowski's list of removals, so do not book it as a starred table. No 2026 review or post found: check it is trading before you plan around it.", "person": "Robert Compagnon & Jessica Yang", "signal_chip": {"label": "Star 2020-22", "full": "Held one MICHELIN star from the 2020 guide; it was among the one-star removals in the 2023 France guide (Gilles Pudlowski, March 2023). Still carried on the World's 50 Best Discovery directory.", "cosign": "Gilles Pudlowski (March 2023); restaurant directory listing"}, "status": "unverified", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "A walnut counter in the 11th where binchotan yakitori and hand-rolled pasta share one tasting menu.", "dishes": [{"name": "Spaghetti with bottarga", "note": "House pasta under grated cured mullet roe; the plate regulars return for", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Yakitori thigh with clementine zest", "note": "Chicken thigh over binchotan, perfumed with citrus", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Grilled oyster", "note": "Oyster cooked on the charcoal with herbs and garlic", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Meatball", "note": "A pork polpette, the Italian half of the menu on a skewer", "kind": "dish"}], "reservation": "Book weeks ahead (listings suggest about six weeks for weekends, four for a Tuesday)", "neighborhood": "11th · Oberkampf"},
    {"id": "v-le-grand-vefour", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 22, "badge": "G&M 14/20 (2026)", "name": "Le Grand Véfour", "short": "Le Grand Véfour", "lat": 48.8657, "lng": 2.3377, "tags": ["1st · Palais-Royal", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Grande table historique · Palais-Royal"], "why": "Guy Martin left at the end of 2025, and Accor's Paris Society, led by Laurent de Gourcuff, reopened the room on 12 May 2026 with Bruno Doucet of La Régalade, the first time the group has built a project around a chef. Doucet put carving and service back at the centre and added a 150-seat terrace in the Palais-Royal garden. Gault&Millau's 2026 review gives it 14/20, saying he does not overturn the house so much as give it a second wind.", "address": "17 Rue de Beaujolais, 75001 Paris, France", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Le+Grand+V%C3%A9four+Paris", "category": "grand", "verdict": "A listed eighteenth-century room under the Palais-Royal arcades, where the banquettes still carry brass plaques for Napoléon, Victor Hugo and Colette. Paris Society reopened it on 12 May 2026 with Bruno Doucet's pleasure-first French menu and a garden terrace; Gault&Millau 2026 rates it 14/20. Go for the room and the ghosts first.", "signature": "Gigot d'agneau de lait carved tableside and a soufflé glacé au Grand Marnier — anchors of Bruno Doucet's pleasure-first, tableside-service menu (Elite Traveler; Les Nouvelles Gastronomiques, 2026). The old-era emblem, Guy Martin's ravioles de foie gras \"Prince Vladimir,\" retired with him at the end of 2025.", "caveat": "No MICHELIN star since the 2021 guide, and the relaunch carries none; Gault&Millau 2026 rates it 14/20 'Table de Chef'. A la carte only, no fixed menu. One listing (Mes Prestiges) names a different chef, Shintaro Awa, against Gault&Millau, Le Figaro and Paris Match naming Doucet, so confirm when you book. Come for the listed room and the garden terrace.", "person": "Bruno Doucet (chef; owns La Régalade Saint-Honoré and Le Comptoir du Relais; trained under Barrier, Vigato and Gagnaire) — owner Paris Society / Laurent de Gourcuff (Accor)", "signal_chip": {"label": "G&M 14/20", "full": "Gault&Millau 2026: 14/20 with a 'Table de Chef' mention for Bruno Doucet's relaunch. No MICHELIN star; the house held three from 2000 to 2008 and two until the 2021 guide.", "cosign": "Gault&Millau 2026"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "The 1784 Palais-Royal dining room, reopened in May 2026 by Paris Society with Bruno Doucet carving at table.", "dishes": [{"name": "Gigot d'agneau de lait", "note": "Milk-fed lamb carved at the table, the centre of Doucet's tableside service", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Soufflé glacé au Grand Marnier", "note": "The iced soufflé Gault&Millau names on the 2026 carte", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Label Rouge gambas", "note": "A starter on the 2026 a la carte list", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Bar fillet", "note": "Sea bass, one of the fish plates Gault&Millau cites", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Call ahead: Mes Prestiges lists lunch and dinner Tuesday to Saturday, to be confirmed when booking; no own-site hours seen", "neighborhood": "1st · Palais-Royal", "hot_this_month": "On rue de Beaujolais under the Palais-Royal arcades: a fitting room for a Beaujolais Nouveau lunch on Thu 19 Nov 2026 (checked 16 Oct 2026)."},
    {"id": "v-guy-savoy", "cat": "shop", "tier": "several", "priority": 3, "badge": "LA LISTE 2026 NO.1", "name": "Guy Savoy", "short": "Guy Savoy", "lat": 48.8572, "lng": 2.3389, "tags": ["6th · Quai de Conti", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Haute cuisine classique"], "why": "Savoy trained three years with the Troisgros brothers in Roanne, opened on rue Duret in 1980, and in 2015 moved into the first floor of the Monnaie de Paris, where coins were struck for the kings. MICHELIN took his third star in 2023 after twenty-one years; La Liste has still put him at No. 1 for nine straight years. The artichoke and black-truffle soup, with a truffle brioche to drag through it, is the plate Gault&Millau's 2026 review still sends you for.", "address": "11 quai de Conti, 75006 Paris, France (Monnaie de Paris / Hotel de la Monnaie)", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Guy+Savoy+Paris", "category": "grand", "verdict": "Two MICHELIN stars (down from three in 2023), yet joint No. 1 on La Liste 2026: a grand classical restaurant inside the Monnaie de Paris, the old royal mint, where the tasting menu still opens with the artichoke and black-truffle soup that has anchored his cooking for decades. The menu now runs to about EUR 740 (seen Jan 2026).", "signature": "Soupe d'artichaut a la truffe noire — artichoke and black-truffle soup, served with a toasted mushroom-and-truffle brioche slicked in black-truffle butter; his longest-standing signature and the plate most often named as his defining dish.", "caveat": "The \"3 stars\" billing is out of date: MICHELIN cut it to two in 2023 (it had held three since 2002) and has not restored the third. Its \"world's best\" standing comes from La Liste's algorithmic aggregator — and in 2026 that top spot is shared with nine other restaurants, all scoring 99.5/100 — not a single live-judged verdict. Dinner is a special-occasion outlay (roughly EUR 490 before wine); open only Wednesday to Saturday, with Saturday dinner-only.", "person": "Guy Savoy (b. 24 July 1953, Nevers) — chef-owner. Apprenticed three years under Pierre and Jean Troisgros at Maison Troisgros in Roanne, then cooked at Lasserre before opening his own Paris table on rue Duret in 1980; moved into the Monnaie de Paris in 2015. His kitchen trained Gordon Ramsay and Richard Ekkebus among others.", "signal_chip": {"label": "La Liste No. 1", "full": "Joint No. 1 restaurant in the world in La Liste 2026, its ninth consecutive year at the top. MICHELIN cut it to two stars in 2023; Yonder (January 2026) gives two stars.", "cosign": "La Liste 2026 (announced December 2025)"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "Guy Savoy's six salons in the old royal mint on the Seine, two MICHELIN stars and La Liste's joint No. 1.", "dishes": [{"name": "Soupe d'artichaut à la truffe noire", "note": "Artichoke and black-truffle soup, his longest-standing signature", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Truffled brioche", "note": "Toasted mushroom and truffle brioche with black-truffle butter, served with the soup", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Peas and lobster", "note": "Yonder's January 2026 review lists it among the house signatures", "kind": "dish"}], "price_range": "Call ahead: Gault&Millau 2026 gives EUR 295-760 on one page and EUR 480-740 on another; Yonder (Jan 2026) lists a menu at EUR 740 (seen Oct 2026)", "neighborhood": "6th · Quai de Conti", "hot_this_month": "Autumn suits the signature artichoke and black-truffle soup with its truffle-butter brioche; La Liste joint No. 1 for 2026, two MICHELIN stars (checked 16 Oct 2026)."},
    {"id": "v-le-train-bleu", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 23, "badge": "G&M 12/20 (2025)", "name": "Le Train Bleu", "short": "Le Train Bleu", "lat": 48.8443, "lng": 2.3736, "tags": ["12th · Gare de Lyon", "Not confirmed for 2026 — call ahead"], "productTags": ["Historic grand brasserie"], "why": "The buffet was built for the 1900 Exposition Universelle and opened the next year; painters of the day covered its walls and ceilings with views of the cities the Paris-Lyon-Méditerranée line ran to, and in 1972 the room was listed as a historic monument. The station caterer SSP later paid for a full restoration of the gilding, stucco and canvases. Since 2018 the menu has been curated by Michel Rostang: lamb carved at the trolley, crêpes Suzette flamed at the table, a rum baba.", "address": "Place Louis Armand, 75012 Paris (1st floor, Gare de Lyon, Hall 1)", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Le+Train+Bleu+Paris", "category": "grand", "verdict": "You come for the room, not the plate. The gilded 1901 dining room above Gare de Lyon — 41 painted panels and crystal chandeliers — was classified a monument historique in 1972, and it is the real event. The food, Michel Rostang's traditional brasserie repertoire since October 2018, is competent and deliberately safe: Gault&Millau rates it 12/20, \"Table Gourmande,\" not a destination kitchen.", "signature": "Gigot d'agneau rôti — leg of lamb carved tableside from the trolley, with gratin dauphinois: the Train Bleu's tableside signature, kept on the card by Rostang. Crêpes Suzette, flambéed tableside, to finish.", "caveat": "Come for the Belle Époque room, not the cooking. Paris Unlocked found it \"a bit safe\" and \"not always particularly inventive,\" with the pike-quenelle's Newburg sauce \"curiously lacking in creaminess.\" Prices track the setting, not the kitchen.", "person": "Michel Rostang has curated the menu since 2018 (the year is disputed between sources); the head chef's name differs between sources, so none is printed.", "signal_chip": {"label": "G&M 12/20 (2025)", "full": "Gault&Millau: 12/20, \"Table Gourmande\" — \"Institution nationale, Le Train Bleu est autant une bonne brasserie qu'un monument,\" where Rostang \"sans prendre trop de risque, assure sa partition\" (fr.gaultmillau.com, 2025).", "cosign": "Gault&Millau"}, "status": "unverified", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "The gilded 1901 buffet above Gare de Lyon's Hall 1, with lamb carved from a trolley under 41 painted panels.", "dishes": [{"name": "Gigot d'agneau rôti", "note": "Roast leg of lamb carved tableside from the trolley, with gratin dauphinois", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Crêpes Suzette", "note": "Flambéed at the table", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Baba au rhum", "note": "The rum baba listings name among the house classics", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Quenelle de brochet, sauce Newburg", "note": "Pike quenelle; reviewers have found the sauce short on cream", "kind": "dish"}], "price_range": "Call ahead: Time Out lists set menus at EUR 56, 70 and 98; Paris-friendly lists Menu du Voyageur EUR 57, PLM EUR 78, Train Bleu EUR 120 (+ wine EUR 42) (seen Oct 2026, both undated)", "phone": "+33 1 43 43 09 06", "neighborhood": "12th · Gare de Lyon"},
    {"id": "v-septime", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 8, "badge": "50 BEST NO. 40", "name": "Septime", "short": "Septime", "lat": 48.8534, "lng": 2.3805, "tags": ["11th · Charonne", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Neo-bistro / modern French"], "why": "Bertrand Grébaut, ex-Arpège, opened Septime in 2011 with his school friend Théophile Pourriat, who runs the floor and the wine; it took a MICHELIN star in 2014. Grébaut died in early July 2026, aged 44, after a long illness; Pourriat announced it on the restaurant's Instagram on 4 July. Gault&Millau's 2026 review says the house remains singular without him, still serving a hidden menu that turns with the seasons.", "address": "80 rue de Charonne, 75011 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Septime+Paris", "category": "neo", "verdict": "One MICHELIN star and No. 40 on The World's 50 Best 2025, and lunch is a carte blanche you do not choose: the kitchen sends what the week's harvest decides. The neo-bistro that set the template for a generation of Paris kitchens, in its first season without its founding chef, and still one of the hardest of them to book.", "signature": "No fixed signature dish, by design: the carte blanche tasting menu is the whole offer, five steps at lunch, seven at dinner, driven by a near-obsessive micro-seasonality. Recent plates cited by The World's 50 Best: cepes and girolles in peppercorn sauce, and young leeks in a mussel-and-pimenton butter under wafer-thin lardo.", "caveat": "Chef-founder Bertrand Grébaut died in July 2026; who now leads the kitchen was not found, so the cooking you read about may change. No a la carte, carte blanche only. Booking is the hard part, and service days differ by source.", "person": "Co-founded in 2011 by Bertrand Grébaut (ex-Arpège under Alain Passard) and Théophile Pourriat. Grébaut died in early July 2026, aged 44 (AFP, 4 July 2026); Pourriat runs the house, and no source yet names who now leads the kitchen.", "signal_chip": {"label": "50 Best No. 40", "full": "No. 40 on The World's 50 Best Restaurants 2025 (the 2026 list is announced 4 November 2026 in Lima); one MICHELIN star, held since 2014.", "cosign": "The World's 50 Best Restaurants 2025; MICHELIN Guide"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "The rue de Charonne room that set the neo-bistro template, now cooking on after its founder chef's death.", "dishes": [{"name": "Carte blanche", "note": "The only offer: five steps at lunch, seven at dinner, written by the week's harvest", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Cèpes and girolles in peppercorn sauce", "note": "An autumn plate cited by The World's 50 Best", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Young leeks in mussel and pimentón butter, lardo", "note": "Leeks under wafer-thin lardo, cited by The World's 50 Best", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Call ahead: one 2026 source gives Tue-Fri lunch 12:15-14:00 and dinner 19:30-22:00 plus Saturday dinner, closed Sun-Mon; another gives closed Saturday and Sunday", "reservation": "Online only; a May 2026 review says the window opens 21 days out at 10:00 Paris time and fills in minutes, another source says one month ahead", "neighborhood": "11th · Charonne", "hot_this_month": "The seasonal menu's moment: The World's 50 Best has cited its cèpes and girolles in peppercorn sauce, and October is cèpe season; book weeks out (checked 16 Oct 2026)."},
    {"id": "v-le-servan", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 12, "badge": "LE FOODING 2015", "name": "Le Servan", "short": "Le Servan", "lat": 48.8603, "lng": 2.3801, "tags": ["11th · Saint-Maur", "Not confirmed for 2026 — call ahead"], "productTags": ["Franco-Filipino neo-bistro"], "why": "Tatiana Levha cooked at L'Arpège and L'Astrance before she and her sister Katia, who runs the floor and the wine, took over a worn corner bar on rue Saint-Maur in 2014 and left its painted ceiling alone. The card is looser than most Paris neo-bistros, a run of small plates rather than a set menu: blood-sausage wontons with sweet chili, lobster ravioli with Sichuan pepper, a tartare with peanuts and Thai chili.", "address": "32 rue Saint-Maur, 75011 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Le+Servan+Paris", "category": "neo", "verdict": "Go at lunch or for the late-afternoon zakouski and order the boudin-noir wontons under sweet chili and the chili-and-Thai-basil mussels; if the sauces and jus don't read as three-star discipline scaled down to bistro plates, the ex-Arpege / ex-Astrance pedigree is a line on a menu, not a kitchen.", "signature": "Wontons de boudin noir, sauce chili doux (blood-sausage wontons, sweet chili) — the house zakouski, a standout singled out by The Infatuation and given its own dish page by Paris by Mouth.", "caveat": "No new find: a twelve-year-old (opened spring 2014), hard-to-book institution whose Le Fooding best-bistro crown dates to 2015. The room seats under 40, and the 2026 listings disagree on its days (one gives closed Sunday and Monday, Saturday dinner only): call before you go.", "person": "Tatiana & Katia Levha", "signal_chip": {"label": "Fooding 2015", "full": "Named Meilleur Bistrot 2015 by Le Fooding. A Mes Prestiges listing claims a MICHELIN star in 2026; no MICHELIN source confirms it.", "cosign": "Le Fooding"}, "status": "unverified", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "The Levha sisters' bistro in an old rue Saint-Maur bar: zinc, painted ceiling, Filipino-accented plates.", "dishes": [{"name": "Wontons de boudin noir, sauce chili doux", "note": "Fried blood-sausage wontons with sweet chili; the house zakouski", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Mussels with chili and Thai basil", "note": "The other plate the old record names to order", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Lobster ravioli with Sichuan pepper", "note": "Named by Time Out alongside the wontons", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Tartare with peanuts and Thai chili", "note": "A bistro classic tweaked, per Paris by Mouth", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Call ahead: Mes Prestiges (May 2026) gives Tue-Fri 12:00-14:00 and 19:30-22:30, Sat 19:30-22:30, closed Sun-Mon; the old record says Monday to Saturday; Paris by Mouth describes weekday lunch and dinner plus Saturday dinner", "reservation": "Book ahead; one listing says bookings open about a month out and fill quickly", "neighborhood": "11th · Saint-Maur"},
    {"id": "v-clown-bar", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 17, "badge": "INFATUATION 2016", "name": "Clown Bar", "short": "Clown Bar", "lat": 48.863, "lng": 2.3684, "tags": ["11th · Cirque d'Hiver", "Not confirmed for 2026 — call ahead"], "productTags": ["Neo-bistro / natural wine"], "why": "The circus next door sent its clowns and acrobats here to drink; the Sarreguemines ceramic frieze of clowns that wraps the room is why it was listed as a historic monument in 1995. Sven Chartier and Ewen Lemoigne of Saturne took it over in 2014 and hired Sota Atsumi, whose poached calf's brain in cold dashi, added in 2014, has outlived his own departure in 2019 to stay on a card that otherwise keeps changing.", "address": "114 rue Amelot, 75011 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Clown+Bar+Paris", "category": "neo", "verdict": "Order the cervelle de veau in chilled dashi — the one plate that never leaves the constantly-changing menu — and you eat Sota Atsumi's decade-old signature beneath a Sarreguemines ceramic clown frieze classé monument historique since 1995.", "signature": "Cervelle de veau: poached veal brain in a chilled dashi/ponzu broth finished with finely diced ginger and scallion. Added by Atsumi in 2014 and, by every account, the permanent fixture on an otherwise rotating card. The duck-and-foie-gras pithivier — breast, leg and foie gras in a caramelised crust — is the other order-it dish.", "caveat": "Two honest asterisks. The dining room is genuinely tiny and books out, so walk-ins rarely land a seat, and at bistro-plus prices with an offal-forward menu (brain, sweetbreads) this is a splurge, not a casual drop-in. And to correct the brief: Clown Bar is not on The Infatuation's current Best Bistros in Paris guide — its Infatuation review and Eater rave both date to 2016, so the press signal is real but aged.", "person": "Owners Sven Chartier & Ewen Lemoigne (also behind Saturne, 2e), who took over the historic bar in 2014 and installed Japanese chef Sota Atsumi — since departed to open his own Maison (2019). The kitchen has changed hands since; the current chef is reported as Korean-born Jung Yonghoon, though only via secondary listings, not an authority byline.", "signal_chip": {"label": "Press · 2016", "full": "Reviewed by The Infatuation (Dec 2016) and called the most thrilling restaurant in Paris by Eater the same year; carried on World's 50 Best Discovery as a directory listing. Press signal is real but dated.", "cosign": "The Infatuation; Eater (2016)"}, "status": "unverified", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "The Cirque d'Hiver's 1902 bar, ringed by a clown-tile frieze; now a natural-wine bistro serving brain in dashi", "dishes": [{"name": "Cervelle de veau, bouillon dashi", "note": "Poached calf's brain in chilled dashi with ginger and scallion; the fixture on a rotating card", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Duck and foie gras pithivier", "note": "Breast, leg and foie gras in a caramelised crust", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Call ahead: Mes Prestiges (May 2026) gives Mon-Fri 12:00-14:00 and 19:30-22:30, closed Sat-Sun; My Art Guides lists daily opening", "reservation": "Book about two weeks ahead; walk-ins rarely land a seat", "neighborhood": "11th · Cirque d'Hiver"},
    {"id": "v-bistrot-paul-bert", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 16, "badge": "BOURDAIN PICK", "name": "Bistrot Paul Bert", "short": "Bistrot Paul Bert", "lat": 48.8522, "lng": 2.3849, "tags": ["11th · Faidherbe", "Not confirmed for 2026 — call ahead"], "productTags": ["Bistrot classique"], "why": "Bertrand Auboyneau left finance to open the bistro in 1997 with his wife Gwenaëlle Cadoret, and built its cellar on growers' wines; he later added Le 6 Paul Bert a few doors down. The steak au poivre is the reason most people call: a thick cut crusted in cracked pepper, sauced with cognac and cream, with frites. Dorie Greenspan liked it enough to adapt it for her book Around My French Table.", "address": "18 rue Paul Bert, 75011 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Bistrot+Paul+Bert+Paris", "category": "bistro", "verdict": "Still the dino-classic Paris bistrot Anthony Bourdain loved: the steak au poivre - coarse Sarawak pepper pressed into a thick cut of beef, finished in a cognac-cream sauce with crisp frites - is why you come, opened with complimentary gougeres and closed with a Paris-Brest. Falsifiable: it's phone-only and genuinely hard to land, closed Sunday and Monday, the room now runs heavily international, and at about 44 euros the steak divides critics on value - The Infatuation calls the branded merch and truffle-on-everything \"a money grab.\"", "signature": "Steak au poivre - a thick cut of beef crusted in coarsely cracked Sarawak pepper and finished in a cognac-cream sauce, with crisp frites; complimentary gougeres to open and a Paris-Brest to close.", "caveat": "Reservations are by phone only and genuinely hard to land - the kitchen largely stopped answering the phone in fall 2022 as Bourdain-era demand overwhelmed it, and Paris by Mouth dropped it from its recommendations over the reservation chaos; the room now runs heavily international. In The Infatuation (Emily Monaco, 2024) the cooking still wins, but she flags the branded sweatshirts and truffle-on-everything as feeling like \"a money grab\"; on her own blog the same critic rates it good yet no better than similarly priced bistros such as Chez Georges. Steak au poivre runs about 44 euros. Closed Sunday and Monday.", "person": "Bertrand Auboyneau - owner, a former finance man who opened Paul Bert in 1997 with his wife Gwenaelle Cadoret and built it into a standard-bearer for old-school bistro fare and vigneron-driven wine; kitchen led by head chef Thierry Laurent.", "signal_chip": {"label": "Bourdain pick", "full": "On No Reservations, Anthony Bourdain called it one of the best of the old-school, unchanged-by-time Parisian bistros. A 2026 listing claims a Bib Gourmand; no MICHELIN source confirms it.", "cosign": "Anthony Bourdain, No Reservations (via Time Out); The Infatuation (2024)"}, "status": "unverified", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "The 1997 Faidherbe bistro of steak au poivre, gougères and Paris-Brest, booked by phone only.", "dishes": [{"name": "Steak au poivre", "note": "Thick-cut beef in cracked Sarawak pepper, cognac-cream sauce, crisp frites", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Gougères", "note": "Cheese puffs sent out free to open the meal", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Paris-Brest", "note": "Choux ring with praline cream, the dessert to close on", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Tue-Sat 12:00-14:00 and 19:30-23:00, closed Sun-Mon (Mes Prestiges, May 2026; ParisBouge); not checked on an own site", "phone": "+33 1 43 72 24 01", "reservation": "Phone only; persist, it is hard to get through", "neighborhood": "11th · Faidherbe"},
    {"id": "v-chez-l-ami-jean", "cat": "berth", "tier": "berth_top", "priority": 2, "badge": "G&M 13/20 (2026)", "name": "Chez L'Ami Jean", "short": "Chez L'Ami Jean", "lat": 48.8597, "lng": 2.3059, "tags": ["7th · Gros-Caillou", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Basque bistrot / canteen"], "why": "Jégo cooked for a decade with Yves Camdeborde at La Régalade, then in 2002 took over this old Basque canteen near the Invalides. His riz au lait arrives as a single big bowl for the table, with salted-butter caramel and house nougatine on the side to fold in yourself; Time Out has printed the recipe. In autumn and winter the board turns to game. Gault&Millau's 2026 review keeps it at 13/20 but finds the cooking a little on autopilot.", "address": "27 rue Malar, 75007 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Chez+L%27Ami+Jean+Paris", "category": "bistro", "verdict": "A fixture on The Infatuation's \"The Best Bistros In Paris.\" Order the riz au lait: it arrives as one big bowl for the table, the salted-butter caramel and housemade nougatine in separate dishes for you to fold in yourself. In autumn and winter, wild game — roast grouse, wild boar stew — is the reason to book.", "signature": "Riz au lait — vanilla rice pudding served tableside in a large bowl with salted-butter caramel sauce and housemade nougatine on the side to assemble your own", "caveat": "Loud, cramped and expensive — The Infatuation's top ($$$$) tier: the sensory overload is the whole point, not a quiet or budget meal. Game is strictly seasonal (autumn–winter), so out of season you won't find it, and tables are tight — reserve well ahead.", "person": "Stephane Jego", "signal_chip": {"label": "G&M 13/20", "full": "Gault&Millau 2026: 13/20, two toques, 'still reliable' though 'a little on autopilot'. The Infatuation's Best Bistros in Paris guide (9 June 2026, per the old record) also lists it.", "cosign": "Gault&Millau 2026"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "Stéphane Jégo's loud Basque canteen on rue Malar, famous for one shared bowl of rice pudding.", "dishes": [{"name": "Riz au lait", "note": "One bowl for the table, salted-butter caramel and nougatine served apart", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Roast grouse", "note": "Autumn-winter game, when in season", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Wild boar stew", "note": "The other cold-season game dish the old record names", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Tue-Sat 12:00-14:30 and 19:00-23:00 (Mes Prestiges, May 2026); not checked on an own site", "reservation": "Reserve well ahead; tables are tight", "neighborhood": "7th · Gros-Caillou", "hot_this_month": "Game season is open: the record gives autumn and winter wild game, roast grouse and wild boar stew, as the reason to book; reserve ahead (checked 16 Oct 2026).", "charter": {"book": "Reserve well ahead; tables are tight", "warn": "Loud, cramped and expensive — The Infatuation's top ($$$$) tier: the sensory overload is the whole point, not a quiet or budget meal. Game is strictly seasonal (autumn–winter), so out of season you won't find it, and tables are tight — reserve well ahead.", "fit": "Stéphane Jégo's loud Basque canteen on rue Malar, famous for one shared bowl of rice pudding."}},
    {"id": "v-chez-georges", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 19, "badge": "THE INFATUATION", "name": "Chez Georges", "short": "Chez Georges", "lat": 48.8667, "lng": 2.342, "tags": ["2nd · Mail", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Bistrot classique"], "why": "Georges opened the room in 1964 and his family kept it for nearly fifty years; in 2010 Jean-Gabriel de Bueil and Dominique Paul bought it on the promise to change nothing, and they have not. The menu is still written by hand, the salade lyonnaise still comes as a bowl to share, and the chicken is pan-roasted to the season. Gilles Pudlowski went back in March 2026 and found the decor untouched.", "address": "1 rue du Mail, 75002 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Chez+Georges+Paris", "category": "bistro", "verdict": "Old-Paris kept exactly where it was: on rue du Mail since 1964, the menu still handwritten in looping French script, the salade lyonnaise still arriving as a shared bowl heavy with thumb-sized lardons, the foie de veau still landing beside a mound of shoestring frites. Lunch and dinner on weekdays only - dark every Saturday and Sunday, and closed for the August holidays. Weekday, or not at all.", "signature": "Foie de veau - a thick slab of calf's liver - served beside a mound of shoestring frites, after the escargots and a shared bowl of salade lyonnaise heavy with thumb-sized lardons.", "caveat": "Not cheap and not cuddly. The Infatuation rates it $$$$ and calls the wine list \"ultimately overpriced,\" with service that blends \"efficiency, practiced scorn, and flirtiness.\" There is no online booking - you call, re-call, email, or turn up in person - and beware the several unrelated Paris restaurants that share the name (one of them a vegetarian spot); only rue du Mail is this one.", "person": "Jean-Gabriel de Bueil - the hands-on owner-guardian (The Infatuation names him the bistro's \"committed guardian\"); he and Dominique Paul bought Chez Georges in 2010. It was founded in 1964 by its namesake Georges and stayed in the same family until they took over.", "signal_chip": {"label": "Infatuation", "full": "The Infatuation ($$$$): a time-warp bistro with a handwritten menu, Jean-Gabriel de Bueil its 'committed guardian' (review dated 18 March 2026 per the old record; date not re-seen). Gault&Millau 11/20.", "cosign": "The Infatuation, Paris"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "A 1964 weekday bistro on rue du Mail with a handwritten menu, salade lyonnaise and calf's liver.", "dishes": [{"name": "Foie de veau", "note": "A thick slab of calf's liver beside a mound of shoestring frites", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Salade lyonnaise", "note": "A shared bowl heavy with thumb-sized lardons", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Escargots", "note": "The classic opener before the liver", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Pan-roasted chicken", "note": "Changes garnish with the season, per The Infatuation", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Call ahead: Mon-Fri lunch and dinner, closed Sat-Sun and in August (ParisBouge, Pudlowski, Paris by Mouth agree); evening close given as 22:30 or 23:00", "phone": "+33 1 42 60 07 11", "reservation": "No online booking: call, email or go in person", "neighborhood": "2nd · Mail", "charter": {"book": "No online booking: call, email or go in person", "warn": "Not cheap and not cuddly. The Infatuation rates it $$$$ and calls the wine list \"ultimately overpriced,\" with service that blends \"efficiency, practiced scorn, and flirtiness.\" There is no online booking - you call, re-call, email, or turn up in person - and beware the several unrelated Paris restaurants that share the name (one of them a vegetarian spot); only rue du Mail is this one.", "fit": "A 1964 weekday bistro on rue du Mail with a handwritten menu, salade lyonnaise and calf's liver."}},
    {"id": "v-le-baratin", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 15, "badge": "HERMÉ'S REGULAR", "name": "Le Baratin", "short": "Le Baratin", "lat": 48.8731, "lng": 2.3829, "tags": ["20th · Belleville", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Bistrot · natural wine"], "why": "Raquel Carena came from Argentina with no kitchen training and has cooked at this Belleville address since 1987; her partner Philippe 'Pinuche' Pinoteau took over the bottles in 1992, when few Paris cellars bothered with natural wine. Pierre Hermé told Purepeople in December 2025 that he has been eating here for nearly 30 years, and Time Out says he still comes at least every two weeks. In 2026 Isabelle Huppert named it her favourite Paris bistro.", "address": "3 rue Jouye-Rouve, 75020 Paris (Belleville, 20e)", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Le+Baratin+Paris", "category": "wine", "verdict": "Belleville's benchmark natural-wine bistrot: a daily blackboard of offal-forward, deeply personal cooking that Paris food writers credit Raquel Carena with pioneering — bistronomie before it had a name — paired to one of the city's deepest natural-wine cellars (20-plus by the glass, independent-focused). If you want polish, comfort and a warm greeting, this isn't it; if you want the real, unreconstructed thing, it is.", "signature": "Cervelle de veau — poached calf's brain in lemon butter, the most-cited plate on a daily-changing blackboard that also runs to pork-snout terrine, crispy pig's ears and braised meats.", "caveat": "The welcome is the running joke: reviewers warn the front of house can be brusque, The Infatuation (2024) describing an almost comically ornery maître d', and Time Out admitting it has not always found the service the friendliest. The room is small and the blackboard changes daily.", "person": "Raquel Carena — Argentine chef-owner, cooking here since 1987 and widely credited by Paris food writers (Time Out, The Infatuation) with helping invent \"bistronomie.\" Her partner since 1992, Philippe Pinoteau (\"Pinuche\"), an early champion of natural wine, curates the celebrated, independent-focused cellar.", "signal_chip": {"label": "Hermé's table", "full": "Time Out Paris: pastry chef Pierre Hermé visits 'at least every two weeks'; Hermé told Purepeople (23 Dec 2025) he has been coming for nearly 30 years. Time Out rates it 4/5.", "cosign": "Time Out Paris; Purepeople (Dec 2025)"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "Raquel Carena's Belleville blackboard since 1987: offal, braises and a natural-wine cellar", "dishes": [{"name": "Cervelle de veau au beurre citronné", "note": "Poached calf's brain in lemon butter, the most-cited plate on the blackboard", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Terrine de museau de porc", "note": "Pork-snout terrine, a recurring starter", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Oreilles de cochon croustillantes", "note": "Crisp pig's ears from the offal side of the board", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Braise of the day", "note": "Carena's slow-cooked meats change daily; ask what is on", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Natural wine by the glass", "note": "Pinoteau's mostly organic, independent-grower list, 20-plus by the glass", "kind": "drink"}], "hours": "Tue-Fri 12:15-14:00 and 19:30-23:00; Sat 19:30-23:00; closed Sun-Mon (Time Out listing, undated; confirm by phone)", "reservation": "Book by phone a day or two ahead", "neighborhood": "20th · Belleville"},
    {"id": "v-parcelles", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 13, "badge": "GAULT&MILLAU 13/20", "name": "Parcelles", "short": "Parcelles", "lat": 48.8637, "lng": 2.3549, "tags": ["3rd · Haut-Marais", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Bistrot à vins"], "why": "The room is a 1936 café on rue Chapon that was the bistro Taxi Jaune until Sarah Michielsen and Bastien Fidelin took it over in spring 2021. Fidelin, a sommelier trained in Tours, also runs the wine shop opposite, and the list leans to natural and foreign bottles. Gault&Millau's 2026 review keeps it at 13/20 and names Julien Chevallier at the stove; the slate has carried calf's-head carpaccio and gnocchi in sage butter beside the sweetbreads.", "address": "13 rue Chapon, 75003 Paris, France", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Parcelles+Paris", "category": "wine", "verdict": "Not the unranked newcomer the \"hot table\" framing suggests. Five years open (since spring 2021), Parcelles is an established Haut-Marais standard: Time Out still scores it 4/5 and keeps it \"on the podium of Paris's best bistros\" (Sept 2025), Gault&Millau marks it 13/20, and it holds a Le Fooding listing plus a place in the MICHELIN Guide selection (a plate — no Bib or star). The daily plates back the cellar rather than hiding behind it.", "signature": "Ris de veau au beurre noisette (veal sweetbreads in brown butter, with pommes purée) — the plate most consistently singled out across reviews (Time Out 2025, Gault&Millau, and the restaurant's own listings) — though the menu is chalked on a slate and rotates daily with the market, so treat it as emblematic rather than guaranteed.", "caveat": "The slate changes daily, so no dish is guaranteed. Closed weekends. Gault&Millau 2026 names Julien Chevallier as chef; Time Out has named Emmanuel Perrot, so confirm who is cooking before quoting a name. Mains near EUR 40 put it well above bistro prices.", "person": "Sarah Michielsen & Bastien Fidelin (owners; she ex-Temps au Temps, he a natural-wine sommelier ex-Saint Sébastien who trained at Tours). The kitchen chef is disputed across sources — Julien Chevallier (ex-Semilla/Justine) per Le Fooding and Gault&Millau vs. Emmanuel Perrot (ex-Des Terres) per Time Out — so no chef name is asserted.", "signal_chip": {"label": "G&M 13/20", "full": "Gault&Millau 2026: 13/20, calling five years on rue Chapon an ode to bistronomy; Time Out 4/5 (Sept 2025); Opinionated About Dining Casual Europe No. 167 (2025, per Enprimeurclub).", "cosign": "Gault&Millau 2026; Time Out"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "Haut-Marais wine bistro in a 1936 café: a sommelier's cellar and a slate rewritten daily", "dishes": [{"name": "Ris de veau au beurre noisette", "note": "Veal sweetbreads in brown butter with pommes purée, the plate reviews return to", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Carpaccio de tête de veau", "note": "Thin-sliced calf's head, named in 2026 coverage", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Gnocchi au beurre de sauge", "note": "Gnocchi in sage butter from the rotating slate", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "A bottle from the shop opposite", "note": "Natural and foreign wines from Fidelin's cellar", "kind": "drink"}], "hours": "Call ahead: Enprimeurclub gives lunch Tue-Fri; Paris Eater and the old record say closed Sat-Sun; old record gives roughly 12:00-14:00 and 19:00-22:00", "price_range": "Call ahead: Paris Eater lists starters about EUR 19-24, mains EUR 39-45, desserts about EUR 13; Lesrestos estimates EUR 30-50 a head (seen Oct 2026)", "reservation": "Book dinner weeks ahead (Paris Eater)", "neighborhood": "3rd · Haut-Marais", "charter": {"price": "Call ahead: Paris Eater lists starters about EUR 19-24, mains EUR 39-45, desserts about EUR 13; Lesrestos estimates EUR 30-50 a head (seen Oct 2026)", "book": "Book dinner weeks ahead (Paris Eater)", "warn": "The slate changes daily, so no dish is guaranteed. Closed weekends. Gault&Millau 2026 names Julien Chevallier as chef; Time Out has named Emmanuel Perrot, so confirm who is cooking before quoting a name. Mains near EUR 40 put it well above bistro prices.", "fit": "Haut-Marais wine bistro in a 1936 café: a sommelier's cellar and a slate rewritten daily"}},
    {"id": "v-vantre", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 10, "badge": "WS GRAND AWARD", "name": "Vantre", "short": "Vantre", "lat": 48.8685, "lng": 2.3702, "tags": ["11th · Fontaine-au-Roi", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Neo-bistro & wine cellar"], "why": "Marco Pelletier, once a sommelier at Le Bristol and Taillevent, met chef Iacopo Chomel at blind tastings, and in 2016 they opened this room of about 30 seats in the 11th. By 2019 Wine Spectator had given it a Grand Award, its top wine-list honour, more often held by grand hotel dining rooms. The kitchen has since passed from Chomel to Masaki Nagao and, in 2023, to Nagao's second, Satria Vue, whom Gault&Millau's 2026 review names as chef.", "address": "19 rue de la Fontaine au Roi, 75011 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Vantre+Paris", "category": "wine", "verdict": "The cooking is precise and reliably good, but the reason Vantre matters is the cellar: a list that first won a Wine Spectator Grand Award in 2019, in a room this small, and the rare place to drink at that depth without a fine-dining bill.", "signature": "Gnocchi in sage butter", "caveat": "No MICHELIN star or Bib; the Guide lists it. The kitchen has changed hands three times (Chomel, Nagao, Satria Vue since 2023), so the food is less constant than the wine. About 30 seats, weekdays only; book ahead. Cellar figures vary: Gault&Millau says over 3,000 wines, Le Fooding about 5,000 bottles, Wine Spectator far more in the cellar.", "person": "Marco Pelletier (owner-sommelier, ex-Le Bristol/Epicure & Le Taillevent) with co-founding chef Iacopo Chomel", "signal_chip": {"label": "Grand Award", "full": "Wine Spectator Grand Award, first won 2019; 2025's new Paris Grand Award went to Le Bon Georges, and Vantre's current listing was not re-checked. Gault&Millau 2026: 13/20 with a Table de Chef mention.", "cosign": "Wine Spectator (2019); Gault&Millau 2026"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "Weekday wine bistro on Fontaine-au-Roi, run by a former Bristol and Taillevent sommelier", "dishes": [{"name": "Gnocchi in sage butter", "note": "The plate regulars remember from the short French carte", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "A bottle from the list", "note": "More than 3,000 wines (Gault&Millau); ask Pelletier to pour at your budget", "kind": "drink"}], "hours": "Mon-Fri, lunch and dinner; closed Sat-Sun (Gault&Millau and listing extracts; times not checked)", "neighborhood": "11th · Fontaine-au-Roi"},
    {"id": "v-brasserie-lipp", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 21, "badge": "GAULT&MILLAU 12/20", "name": "Brasserie Lipp", "short": "Brasserie Lipp", "lat": 48.8538, "lng": 2.3327, "tags": ["6th · Saint-Germain", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Alsatian brasserie"], "why": "Léonard Lipp, an Alsatian, opened a beer hall here on 27 October 1880 and served cervelat rémoulade; after 1920 Marcellin Cazes made it a literary address and founded the Prix Cazes, still awarded each November with a table at Lipp as part of the prize. Gault&Millau's 2026 review notes the carte has barely moved since the 1950s: oysters, andouillette, blanquette, choucroute. The Bertrand group has owned it since 1990.", "address": "151 boulevard Saint-Germain, 75006 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Brasserie+Lipp+Paris", "category": "brasserie", "verdict": "The food is not the reason to come, and no one honest pretends otherwise. Order the choucroute garnie, take a ground-floor table, and you are eating inside the Saint-Germain of Verlaine, Apollinaire, Hemingway and Mitterrand. Checkable on the ground: the waiters still wear numbered seniority pins, the 1900 dining room is a protected monument historique, and the upstairs is nicknamed l'Enfer — hell, where you are sent to be forgotten.", "signature": "Choucroute garnie — wine-braised sauerkraut with sausages, bacon and steamed potatoes; the house dish since 1880, when the original starter was cervelat rémoulade.", "caveat": "Not a food pilgrimage. The Infatuation (Feb 2025) found the cooking heavy and short on flavour complexity — \"Between the heaviness of the meal and the lack of flavor complexity, they're fine… ultimately, you're here for the legend.\" It reads as a tourist-supported institution; the seating is a class system (ground-floor main room best, the back room is Purgatoire, the upstairs is Enfer), and the house posts signs about proper attire at the door. No MICHELIN star — the signal is journalistic and historical, not gastronomic.", "person": "Owned by the Bertrand family (Groupe Bertrand, Olivier Bertrand) since 1990; founded 27 Oct 1880 by the Alsatian Léonard Lipp, and made a literary salon by Marcellin Cazes, who redesigned it in 1920.", "signal_chip": {"label": "G&M 12/20", "full": "Gault&Millau 2026: 12/20 with a Table Gourmande mention; menu barely changed since the 1950s. The Infatuation (Feb 2025): 'you're here for the legend.'", "cosign": "Gault&Millau 2026; The Infatuation"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "Saint-Germain's 1880 Alsatian brasserie: choucroute, sole meunière, a listed 1900 dining room", "dishes": [{"name": "Choucroute garnie", "note": "The house dish since 1880: sauerkraut, sausages, bacon, potatoes", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Sole meunière", "note": "A traditional order Mes Prestiges (May 2026) puts ahead of the daily specials", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Andouillette", "note": "On Gault&Millau's 2026 list of unchanged classics", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Blanquette de veau", "note": "Another of the 1950s-era classics still on the carte", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Mille-feuille", "note": "The dessert to close on", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Call ahead: Paris je t'aime and Sluurpy give daily 9:00-00:45; MyMenuWeb gives daily 9:00-02:00", "phone": "+33 1 45 48 53 91", "web": "https://www.brasserielipp.fr", "reservation": "Book ahead for weekend evenings; walk-ins risky on busy nights (Mes Prestiges, May 2026)", "neighborhood": "6th · Saint-Germain", "hot_this_month": "The choucroute garnie at a ground-floor table is the cold-evening order; an easy walk from the Musée d'Orsay and its Cassatt show (checked 16 Oct 2026)."},
    {"id": "v-bofinger", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 24, "badge": "THE INFATUATION", "name": "Bofinger", "short": "Bofinger", "lat": 48.8539, "lng": 2.3681, "tags": ["4th · Bastille", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Historic Alsatian brasserie"], "why": "Frédéric Bofinger, an Alsatian, opened a small beer bar here in 1864, and the house still says it was the first in Paris to pour beer on draught, in 1870, a claim the monument record does not make. The room, with its floral glass cupola and Gambrinus in the stained glass, has been listed since 1989 and was opened for guided tours during the Journées du Patrimoine 2026. Groupe Bertrand runs its 280 seats.", "address": "5-7 rue de la Bastille, 75004 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Bofinger+Paris", "category": "brasserie", "verdict": "The oldest Alsatian brasserie in Paris (est. 1864) and, by its own well-repeated claim, the first in the city to pour draught beer (1870); its facades, sign and interior decor — the floral Art Nouveau glass dome and the Gambrinus stained glass among them — have been a listed monument historique since 13 March 1989 (inscrit MH partiellement, per the official Merimee record), and the choucroute, seafood or classic, is still the reason to book.", "signature": "Choucroute de la mer — salmon, haddock, sea bream (daurade) and a prawn fanned around a mound of juniper-and-cumin sauerkraut under a horseradish-spiked beurre blanc (the classic pork choucroute garnie and the fruits de mer seafood tower are the other house anchors).", "caveat": "A 280-seat, tourist-heavy group brasserie (Groupe Bertrand since its 2017 takeover of Groupe Flo); service, noise and value split diners. The 'first draught beer in Paris' line is house lore. Olivier Nasti's 2023 residency (30 March-31 May) is over; he does not run the kitchen.", "person": "Frédéric Bofinger (Alsatian founder, 1864); today owned by Groupe Bertrand", "signal_chip": {"label": "The Infatuation", "full": "The Infatuation (16 Apr 2025): 'Bofinger is the prototypical brasserie, with a stunning seafood menu that matches the grandeur of the space.'", "cosign": "The Infatuation, Apr 2025"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "Bastille's 1864 Alsatian brasserie under a glass cupola: choucroute of pork or of fish", "dishes": [{"name": "Choucroute de la mer", "note": "Salmon, haddock, sea bream and a prawn over juniper sauerkraut with beurre blanc; the house specialty", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Choucroute garnie", "note": "The pork version, Alsace straight", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Plateau de fruits de mer", "note": "The seafood tower reviewers single out", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Daily 12:00-15:00 and 18:30-23:30, to 00:30 Fri-Sat (Mes Prestiges, May 2026; confirm)", "phone": "+33 1 42 72 87 82 (listing site; not checked against own site)", "neighborhood": "4th · Bastille", "hot_this_month": "Choucroute weather from the clocks going back on 25 Oct: the oldest Alsatian brasserie in Paris, est. 1864, near the Bastille (checked 16 Oct 2026)."},
    {"id": "v-la-coupole", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 25, "badge": "TIME OUT 3/5", "name": "La Coupole", "short": "La Coupole", "lat": 48.8423, "lng": 2.328, "tags": ["14th · Montparnasse", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Grande brasserie Art Deco (Montparnasse)"], "why": "Ernest Fraux and René Lafon, brothers-in-law, opened it on 20 December 1927 and had 27 artists paint the pillars of the dining hall, whose ground floor has been listed since 1988. The lamb curry has stayed on the carte the whole time, said to follow a Madras recipe. Lesrestos reports the house has since asked chef Manoj Sharma to rework it, added a vegetarian curry and sells both to take away. Mitterrand kept table 82.", "address": "102 boulevard du Montparnasse, 75014 Paris, France", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=La+Coupole+Paris", "category": "brasserie", "verdict": "You come for the room, not the cooking: the 1927 dining hall, its pillars hand-painted by 27 artists, has its ground-floor room listed (inscrit) as a monument historique by order of 12 January 1988, and the one dish worth ordering is the Curry d'Agneau, on the menu essentially unchanged since opening day. Verify by walking in: read the columns, then the carte.", "signature": "Curry d'Agneau a l'Indienne — the house lamb curry, on the menu since 1927 and said to follow a Madras recipe; long served tableside \"a la roulotte\" and plated with rice and a spread of condiments (mango chutney, achars). The finer garnish details vary by telling, so take them lightly.", "caveat": "This is an institution living partly on its legend. Curated reviews (The Infatuation, Paris-Bistro) are candid that the food is competent-but-unremarkable grand-brasserie fare and that you are paying for the setting and the history; service can visibly buckle under big group tables at peak. Treat it as a story and a room first, a meal second — and note the fabled \"Mitterrand's last meal\" curry line is repeated lore, not documented fact (what is documented is that Mitterrand kept table 82 and ordered the curry).", "person": "Owned since 2018 by Groupe Bertrand (Olivier Bertrand), one of France's largest restaurant groups, which also runs Angelina and the former Flo brasseries. Earlier it belonged to Jean-Paul Bucher's Groupe Flo (from the late 1980s; the financier Albert Frere's group was a later stakeholder). Opened 20 December 1927 by brothers-in-law Ernest Fraux and Rene Lafon. No single named head chef is reliably documented in the sources reviewed.", "signal_chip": {"label": "Time Out 3/5", "full": "Time Out Paris rates it 3/5: 'the grandest of grand Parisian brasseries', a classic experience without spending too much. Ground-floor room inscrit monument historique, 12 Jan 1988.", "cosign": "Time Out Paris"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "Montparnasse's 1927 Art Deco hall of painted pillars, and the lamb curry served since opening", "dishes": [{"name": "Curry d'agneau à l'indienne", "note": "The 1927 lamb curry with rice and condiments, reworked by Manoj Sharma", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Vegetarian curry", "note": "The newer meat-free curry added alongside the lamb", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Oysters and seafood platters", "note": "The Infatuation's advice: skip the carte, eat shellfish at the bar", "kind": "dish"}], "price_range": "Time Out lists mains EUR 27-36 and set menus EUR 29 and 34 (page undated; seen Oct 2026)", "reservation": "Recommended; it gets very busy", "neighborhood": "14th · Montparnasse"},
    {"id": "v-bouillon-chartier", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 20, "badge": "MICHELIN GUIDE", "name": "Bouillon Chartier", "short": "Bouillon Chartier", "lat": 48.8718, "lng": 2.3434, "tags": ["9th · Grands Boulevards", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Bouillon"], "why": "Frédéric and Camille Chartier opened it in 1896 as a bouillon, a cheap meat-and-broth canteen for working Paris, and the hall with its globe lamps was classé monument historique in 1989. The Joulie family bought it in 2007 and has since put the name on further rooms at Montparnasse and Gare de l'Est. The flagship still takes no bookings, serves without a break from late morning to midnight, and the waiter adds up your bill in pen on the tablecloth.", "address": "7 rue du Faubourg Montmartre, 75009 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Bouillon+Chartier+Paris", "category": "bouillon", "verdict": "A real 1896 broth canteen, not a themed reproduction: strangers still share communal tables under globe lamps while bow-tied waiters tot up the bill on the paper tablecloth, inside a hall whose décor has been classé monument historique since 1989. Mains sit in the low tens of euros — you come for the room and the almost-anachronistic price, not for cooking that would trouble a star.", "signature": "The emblematic cheap opener is the œuf mayonnaise, ordered in loose change; bœuf bourguignon and steak frites (pièce du boucher, sauce poivre, frites) are the house mains, with the chocolate profiterole — called Chartier's crown jewel — the long-running favourite to finish.", "caveat": "It is a tourist institution that feeds hundreds at a clip: expect a queue at the Grands Boulevards flagship (no reservations — first-come, first-served), brisk-to-rushed service, and simple, sometimes uneven cooking. Treat it as living heritage and a genuine bargain, not a destination meal.", "person": "Founded by brothers Frédéric and Camille Chartier (1896); owned since 2007 by the Joulie family / Groupe Joulie, led by director general Christophe Joulie.", "signal_chip": {"label": "MICHELIN Guide", "full": "Named in the MICHELIN Guide's editorial feature 'The Return of Brasseries and Bouillons in Paris' among the city's landmark bouillons, alongside Bouillon Julien and Bouillon Pigalle — an editorial mention, not a star rating.", "cosign": "MICHELIN Guide (guide.michelin.com)"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "The 1896 broth hall on Faubourg Montmartre: shared tables, the bill written on the paper cloth", "dishes": [{"name": "Oeuf mayonnaise", "note": "The opener ordered in loose change", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Boeuf bourguignon", "note": "The house braise among the mains", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Steak frites, sauce poivre", "note": "Pièce du boucher with pepper sauce and fries", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Profiteroles au chocolat", "note": "The long-running favourite to finish", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Daily 11:30-24:00, continuous (Mes Prestiges, May 2026; Paris je t'aime)", "reservation": "No reservations; queue", "price_range": "Call ahead: one guide gives starters from about EUR 1, mains from EUR 7, desserts from EUR 2; another puts a main around EUR 15 (seen Oct 2026)", "neighborhood": "9th · Grands Boulevards", "charter": {"price": "Call ahead: one guide gives starters from about EUR 1, mains from EUR 7, desserts from EUR 2; another puts a main around EUR 15 (seen Oct 2026)", "book": "No reservations; queue", "warn": "It is a tourist institution that feeds hundreds at a clip: expect a queue at the Grands Boulevards flagship (no reservations — first-come, first-served), brisk-to-rushed service, and simple, sometimes uneven cooking. Treat it as living heritage and a genuine bargain, not a destination meal.", "fit": "The 1896 broth hall on Faubourg Montmartre: shared tables, the bill written on the paper cloth"}},
    {"id": "v-polidor", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 26, "badge": "GAULT&MILLAU 11/20", "name": "Polidor", "short": "Polidor", "lat": 48.8489, "lng": 2.339, "tags": ["6th · Odéon", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Old-Paris crémerie-restaurant"], "why": "It opened as a crémerie in 1845 and became a restaurant around 1890; the long shared tables and old fittings are the ones Woody Allen filmed for Midnight in Paris in 2011. Each weekday has its own plat, the 'semainier', so the dish changes with the day of the week rather than the season. Gault&Millau's 2026 review prices a meal at EUR 15.90 to 25 before drinks and praises the house wine by the glass or pitcher.", "address": "41 rue Monsieur-le-Prince, 75006 Paris, France", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Polidor+Paris", "category": "bouillon", "verdict": "A living Belle Epoque dining room, not a great kitchen. The boeuf bourguignon and chocolate mousse are honest, generous and cheap, but the ceiling is real: Gault&Millau stops at 11/20 and plates can arrive lukewarm. Come for permanence and the literary ghosts, not for cooking that changes your mind.", "signature": "Boeuf bourguignon, the emblematic slow-braised bistro main, cheap and generously portioned; for dessert, Gault&Millau singles out the chocolate mousse, with tarte Tatin the other house classic on the menu (Gault&Millau; restaurant menu).", "caveat": "This is sincerity, not spectacle, and the record is genuinely mixed: cooking that can land lukewarm and inconsistent, and brisk, no-nonsense service. It has been tourist-heavy since the 2011 film, seating is elbow-to-elbow at communal tables with shared salt, pepper and mustard, and the WC is still a Turkish squat. You are paying for the room and the history more than the plate.", "signal_chip": {"label": "Gault&Millau 11/20", "full": "Gault&Millau's current online listing scores Polidor 11/20 and calls it a mythical, preserved 'slice of Paris' serving bistro classics revisited for the 21st century, singling out the chocolate mousse; the modest score is itself the tell.", "cosign": "Gault&Millau"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "An 1845 crémerie on rue Monsieur-le-Prince, still serving bourguignon at long shared tables", "dishes": [{"name": "Boeuf bourguignon", "note": "The house reference, slow-braised and cheap", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Le semainier", "note": "The weekday plat du jour, a different dish each day", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Mousse au chocolat", "note": "The dessert Gault&Millau singles out", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Tarte Tatin", "note": "The other house dessert classic", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "House wine by the pichet", "note": "Gault&Millau notes the house wines by glass or pitcher", "kind": "drink"}], "hours": "Call ahead: Rankeat gives daily 12:00-22:30 continuous; Frommer's gives 12:00-15:00 and 19:00-24:00", "phone": "+33 1 43 26 95 34", "price_range": "EUR 15.90-25 a head before drinks (Gault&Millau 2026; seen Oct 2026)", "neighborhood": "6th · Odéon"},
    {"id": "v-bouillon-pigalle", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 30, "badge": "OEUF MAYO CHAMPION", "name": "Bouillon Pigalle", "short": "Bouillon Pigalle", "lat": 48.8824, "lng": 2.3372, "tags": ["18th · Pigalle", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Bouillon — the 19th-century workers' canteen, revived"], "why": "Pierre-Louis Duval's bouillons of the 1850s fed the porters of Les Halles on broth and boiled beef, and by the late twentieth century almost none were left. Pierre and Guillaume Moussié opened this 300-seat room near the Moulin Rouge in 2017 to show the sums still worked, and in June 2019 its egg mayonnaise, made under chef Clément Chicard, was named world champion by the ASOM jury. Gault&Millau's 2026 review puts the bourguignon at EUR 12.20.", "address": "22 boulevard de Clichy, 75018 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Bouillon+Pigalle+Paris", "category": "bouillon", "verdict": "Order the oeuf mayo, a beef bourguignon (EUR 12.20 by Gault&Millau 2026) and profiteroles, add a pichet, and you are around EUR 20 to 25 a head in a 300-seat room on the boulevard de Clichy.", "signature": "Oeuf mayonnaise: the plain egg and mayonnaise whose recipe, under chef Clément Chicard, was crowned world champion by the ASOM jury in June 2019.", "caveat": "Go for the room, the roar and the arithmetic, not for finesse. This is cooking that is generous, not precise: competent bistro classics turned out at canteen speed for a 300-seat room. It now takes bookings for parties of two to twelve — a break from its walk-in-only start — but the queue down boulevard de Clichy is still real after 7pm, and on an off night it reads more tourist crush than neighbourhood table.", "person": "Pierre & Guillaume Moussié", "signal_chip": {"label": "Oeuf mayo 2019", "full": "Its oeuf mayonnaise was named world champion by the ASOM jury in June 2019; Gault&Millau 2026: a correct meal for about EUR 20; featured in MICHELIN's bouillon-revival article (editorial, no star or Bib).", "cosign": "ASOM (2019); Gault&Millau 2026; MICHELIN Guide feature"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "The 2017 bouillon revival on boulevard de Clichy: 300 seats, noon to midnight, bourguignon at EUR 12", "dishes": [{"name": "Oeuf mayonnaise", "note": "The 2019 ASOM world-champion egg mayonnaise", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Boeuf bourguignon", "note": "EUR 12.20 per Gault&Millau 2026", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Terrine de canard", "note": "Duck terrine, EUR 5.40 (Gault&Millau 2026)", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Île flottante", "note": "Floating island, EUR 3.20 (Gault&Millau 2026)", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Profiteroles", "note": "The house finish; a 2024 review found them big but light on filling", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Daily 12:00-24:00, continuous (Mes Prestiges, May 2026; Privateaser)", "reservation": "Call ahead: Privateaser takes online bookings (groups over 12 by email or phone); Mes Prestiges (May 2026) says it runs mostly walk-in", "price_range": "About EUR 20 a head (Gault&Millau 2026; seen Oct 2026)", "phone": "+33 1 42 59 69 31 (Mes Prestiges; not checked against own site)", "neighborhood": "18th · Pigalle", "charter": {"price": "About EUR 20 a head (Gault&Millau 2026; seen Oct 2026)", "book": "Call ahead: Privateaser takes online bookings (groups over 12 by email or phone); Mes Prestiges (May 2026) says it runs mostly walk-in", "warn": "Go for the room, the roar and the arithmetic, not for finesse. This is cooking that is generous, not precise: competent bistro classics turned out at canteen speed for a 300-seat room. It now takes bookings for parties of two to twelve — a break from its walk-in-only start — but the queue down boulevard de Clichy is still real after 7pm, and on an off night it reads more tourist crush than neighbourhood table.", "fit": "The 2017 bouillon revival on boulevard de Clichy: 300 seats, noon to midnight, bourguignon at EUR 12"}},
    {"id": "v-clamato", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 29, "badge": "BIB GOURMAND", "name": "Clamato", "short": "Clamato", "lat": 48.8536, "lng": 2.3805, "tags": ["11th · Charonne", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["No-reservations seafood bar"], "why": "Bertrand Grébaut and Théophile Pourriat opened it in November 2013 beside Septime and named it after the Québécois clam-and-tomato cocktail. They have never taken a booking, which MICHELIN's inspectors, who give it a Bib Gourmand, sum up as first come, first served. The fish comes from boats at Saint-Jean-de-Luz, Concarneau and the Île d'Yeu, and the Canadian thread ends with dessert: a maple-syrup tart.", "address": "80 rue de Charonne, 75011 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Clamato+Paris", "category": "day", "verdict": "The Septime team's raw bar is the 11e's benchmark for catch-of-the-day seafood, and it has never taken a booking since it opened in 2013 — turn up at the 7pm opening or after 10pm, or you queue for a stool.", "signature": "Couteaux, beurre d'herbes — razor clams in herb butter, finished with almonds. The catch changes with the morning's delivery, but the raw-bar oysters, crab accras, the fruits de mer platter (50 Best's \"star of the show\") and a Québécois maple-syrup pie are the constants.", "caveat": "No reservations, ever, and the room is small and shoulder-to-shoulder — expect to wait unless you arrive at opening or late. The menu is dictated by the day's catch, so any single dish may not be on; and while it wears a Bib Gourmand, platters and natural wine make the bill climb. Listed hours conflict across sources (some show closures early in the week) — verify before going.", "person": "Opened in November 2013 by Bertrand Grébaut and Théophile Pourriat as Septime's no-bookings seafood sibling; Grébaut died in July 2026 and Pourriat runs the house.", "signal_chip": {"label": "Bib Gourmand", "full": "MICHELIN Bib Gourmand, listed under the France 2026 selection (Mes Prestiges extract; Pearl dates it 2025). MICHELIN's page: no reservations, first come, first served.", "cosign": "MICHELIN Guide"}, "reservation": "None; first come, first served (MICHELIN)", "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "Septime's no-bookings seafood bar on rue de Charonne; the day boats write the slate", "dishes": [{"name": "Couteaux, beurre d'herbes", "note": "Razor clams in herb butter with almonds", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Oysters from the raw bar", "note": "The constant on a menu set by the catch", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Accras de crabe", "note": "Crab fritters", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Plateau de fruits de mer", "note": "The platter 50 Best called the star of the show", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Tarte au sirop d'érable", "note": "Québécois maple-syrup tart", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Call ahead: Mes Prestiges (2026) gives Tue-Sat 12:00-14:30 and 19:00-22:30, closed Sun-Mon; other listings show different early-week closures", "neighborhood": "11th · Charonne", "hot_this_month": "R-month oyster season: the raw-bar oysters and the fruits de mer platter are the order; no bookings, so queue at opening (checked 16 Oct 2026).", "charter": {"book": "None; first come, first served (MICHELIN)", "warn": "No reservations, ever, and the room is small and shoulder-to-shoulder — expect to wait unless you arrive at opening or late. The menu is dictated by the day's catch, so any single dish may not be on; and while it wears a Bib Gourmand, platters and natural wine make the bill climb. Listed hours conflict across sources (some show closures early in the week) — verify before going.", "fit": "Septime's no-bookings seafood bar on rue de Charonne; the day boats write the slate"}},
    {"id": "v-mokonuts", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 11, "badge": "PARIS BY MOUTH", "name": "Mokonuts", "short": "Mokonuts", "lat": 48.8508, "lng": 2.3814, "tags": ["11th · Aligre", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Weekday breakfast & lunch day kitchen - Franco-Levantine-Japanese"], "why": "Moko Hirayama left law and finance; Omar Koreitem had cooked at Daniel in New York and the Savoy Grill in London. In 2015 they opened this small room near the Aligre market. Paris by Mouth's 2026 revisit notes that ten years on they have chosen not to make it bigger: still about twenty seats, still shut in the afternoon so they can collect their daughters. The growth went elsewhere, into two offshoots, Locomal and the all-day café Mokochaya.", "address": "5 rue Saint-Bernard, 75011 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Mokonuts+Paris", "category": "day", "verdict": "A 20-seat, breakfast-and-lunch-only room open Monday to Friday since 2015, where Moko Hirayama runs the pastry and floor and Omar Koreitem cooks one short, market-driven Franco-Levantine menu. It holds no Michelin star and only a modest Gault&Millau 12/20 (2026) - the real signal is that it books up weeks in advance on critical praise alone.", "signature": "Moko Hirayama's cookies - American-style bakes rerouted through Japan with miso, black sesame and tahini (the black-olive-and-white-chocolate and peanut-butter-milk-chocolate are the cult orders, per Paris by Mouth and David Lebovitz); on the savory side, the short daily menu has run to hake with pil-pil sauce and caramelized endive (Paris by Mouth). The menu changes with the market.", "caveat": "Breakfast and lunch only, weekdays, and just 20 covers - it books up weeks ahead (The Infatuation lists it among Paris's toughest reservations), so walk-ins are near-hopeless. The Infatuation pegs it $$$$. If the table won't happen, come for the cookies.", "person": "Moko Hirayama & Omar Koreitem", "signal_chip": {"label": "Paris by Mouth", "full": "Paris by Mouth, 'Mokonuts, revisited' (2026): rated GREAT, on its 50 Favorites since the list began. The Infatuation (18 Mar 2026): 'flavor-packed and near-perfect.' Gault&Millau 12/20.", "cosign": "Paris by Mouth 2026; The Infatuation 2026"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "Ten years and about 20 seats near Aligre: Omar Koreitem's lunch and Moko Hirayama's cookies", "dishes": [{"name": "Black-olive and white-chocolate cookie", "note": "One of Hirayama's two cult cookies", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Peanut-butter and milk-chocolate cookie", "note": "The other cult order", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Miso, black sesame or tahini cookies", "note": "American bakes routed through Japan", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Hake, pil-pil sauce, caramelised endive", "note": "A past lunch plate Paris by Mouth described; the menu changes with the market", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Call ahead: Mes Prestiges (May 2026) and Time Out give Mon-Fri 8:45-18:00, closed Sat-Sun; Paris by Mouth (2026) describes weekday lunch only; Mes Prestiges also mentions occasional dinners", "reservation": "Book weeks ahead; walk-ins near hopeless", "neighborhood": "11th · Aligre"},
    {"id": "v-l-as-du-fallafel", "cat": "berth", "tier": "berth_top", "priority": 3, "badge": "INFATUATION PICK", "name": "L'As du Fallafel", "short": "L'As du Fallafel", "lat": 48.8573, "lng": 2.3596, "tags": ["4th · Marais", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["Falafel window - Marais Jewish quarter"], "why": "Isaac and Daisy Peretz opened it in 1979 on rue des Rosiers, the spine of the Marais's old Jewish quarter, and the family still runs it. Mark Bittman called it, in The New York Times, the falafel destination of Paris and of Europe; Lenny Kravitz's praise is posted in the window. It is kosher under the Beth Din of Paris, so it closes from Friday afternoon through Saturday, and rival windows on the same street now claim its title.", "address": "34 rue des Rosiers, 75004 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=L%27As+du+Fallafel+Paris", "category": "window", "verdict": "Order and pay at the green window before you even reach the front, then walk off with a fresh pita crammed with six herb-green falafel balls, silky fried aubergine, tahini, and shredded red-and-white cabbage - heavy as two fists, and the falafel every rival window on rue des Rosiers is measured against.", "signature": "The falafel special (pita): six crisp, herb-dense falafel balls so green they match the storefront, plus fried aubergine, tahini, hummus, cabbage and cucumber.", "caveat": "The queue is real and regularly spills into the street. It is kosher (Beth Din of Paris oversight) and therefore closed Friday evening, all day Saturday and on Jewish holidays for Shabbat, so plan around it. And the \"best falafel\" crown is genuinely contested: rue des Rosiers is now lined with rival windows all claiming it (Vice documented an outright \"falafel war\"), and the signal here is journalistic, not a MICHELIN or Gault&Millau ranking.", "person": "Founded 1979 by Isaac and Daisy Peretz (per press/trade profiles - hedge: this founder detail comes from journalism/travel features, not a culinary authority); still family-run, associated with son Yomi Peretz. A longtime favourite of Lenny Kravitz, whose recommendation is displayed on the storefront (Forbes, 2014).", "signal_chip": {"label": "Infatuation", "full": "The Infatuation (8 Oct 2025): six falafel 'as green as the storefront' with fried eggplant and tahini. Mark Bittman (NYT): 'the falafel destination in Paris, indeed in Europe.'", "cosign": "The Infatuation, 2025"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "Rue des Rosiers' green-fronted falafel window since 1979, kosher and shut for Shabbat", "dishes": [{"name": "Falafel special in pita", "note": "Six herb-green falafel with fried aubergine, tahini, hummus, cabbage and cucumber", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Fried aubergine", "note": "The silky slices that make the special; ask for extra", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Call ahead: Sun-Thu 11:00-24:00 (Mes Prestiges, May 2026) or 11:00-23:30 (Paris je t'aime); Fri 11:00-17:00 (both); closed Saturday and Jewish holidays", "neighborhood": "4th · Marais"},
    {"id": "v-chez-alain-miam-miam", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 28, "badge": "THE INFATUATION", "name": "Chez Alain Miam Miam", "short": "Chez Alain Miam Miam", "lat": 48.8618, "lng": 2.3646, "tags": ["3rd · Haut-Marais", "Not confirmed for 2026 — call ahead"], "productTags": ["Sandwicherie / griddled sandwiches"], "why": "Alain built his name griddling sandwiches at a stall in the Marché des Enfants Rouges, then opened his own counter at 26 rue Charlot, which Newtable dates to June 2018. Every order is layered by hand at the hotplate. On the night of 11 to 12 October 2026 fire destroyed the covered market and 17 of its businesses; reports name Alain as one of its best-known traders but do not say what became of his stall. The rue Charlot shop is a separate address.", "address": "26 rue Charlot, 75003 Paris (the shop only; the former market stall is gone with the market)", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Chez+Alain+Miam+Miam+Paris", "category": "window", "verdict": "A two-handed, overstuffed toasted sandwich, layered and pressed to order at the counter. Order the smoky cecina de Leon with Cantal (or Comte), the complimentary tomato, caramelized onion and mushroom, and have it toasted. Budget from 13.50 euros, plus roughly 25 minutes in line just to reach the counter.", "signature": "The oversized griddled sandwich: smoky cecina de Leon (or cooked/raw ham, pastrami, spiced chicken, smoked trout), Cantal or Comte, plus tomato, caramelized onion and mushroom, toasted to order. Buckwheat galettes round out the board.", "caveat": "The Marché des Enfants Rouges burned down on 11-12 October 2026 (Time Out Paris, AFP): do not go to the market. No report found on whether the rue Charlot shop is trading after the fire; call or check its social accounts first. When open: The Infatuation calls the EUR 13.50-plus sandwich overhyped and underseasoned, with about 25 minutes in line to order.", "signal_chip": {"label": "The Infatuation", "full": "Reviewed by The Infatuation's Paris team: a Marche des Enfants Rouges original that moved to its own rue Charlot storefront, with a specific recommended build (smoky cecina, Cantal or Comte, tomato, caramelized onion, mushroom, toasted).", "cosign": "The Infatuation (Paris), mixed review: praises the toasted cecina build but calls the 13.50-euro panini 'overhyped and overpriced.'"}, "status": "unverified", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "hook": "Alain's rue Charlot counter: two-handed toasted sandwiches built to order; check it is open", "dishes": [{"name": "Cecina de León sandwich", "note": "Smoked cured beef with Cantal or Comté, tomato, caramelised onion, mushroom, toasted", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Pastrami sandwich", "note": "One of the alternative fillings on the board", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Smoked trout sandwich", "note": "The fish option", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Buckwheat galette", "note": "The board's other line", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Call ahead: Mes Prestiges (May 2026) gives Tue-Sun 12:00-17:00, closed Mon; Le Marais Mood gives Tue-Sun 9:00-17:00", "price_range": "From EUR 13.50 a sandwich (The Infatuation; seen Oct 2026)", "neighborhood": "3rd · Haut-Marais"},
    {"id": "v-le-petit-vendome", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 36, "badge": "TIME OUT NO.1 2025", "name": "Le Petit Vendôme", "short": "Le Petit Vendôme", "neighborhood": "2nd · Opéra", "tags": ["2nd · Opéra", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["jambon-beurre", "sandwich", "bistro"], "why": "In 2025 Time Out's writers ranked this jambon-beurre first on their world sandwich list. Gilles Caussade, who took over the bistro, says he changed nothing: the yellow-and-red formica stays, as does the build of Julien bakery baguette, fresh butter and ham cut from the bone in front of you. AFP reported in April 2026 that barriers now hold the queue on the pavement and about 400 sandwiches go out a day, from morning to midnight, at EUR 6.50.", "address": "8 rue des Capucines, 75002 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Le+Petit+Vend%C3%B4me+Paris", "category": "window", "verdict": "The genuine benchmark, not a tourist trap dressed as one: order it plain to judge it. The classic jambon-beurre runs about EUR 6.50 (AFP, Apr 2026), and it is closed on Sundays.", "signature": "The jambon-beurre, plain, to read the bread, butter and ham balance; or the Spécial, with dry-cured country ham, fresh goat cheese and olive oil.", "person": "", "caveat": "Since the Time Out ranking the queue is managed with barriers on the pavement; go early or late. Counter service, no reservations; closed Sunday.", "hook": "Time Out's best sandwich in the world, 2025: a jambon-beurre made to order near Vendôme", "signal_chip": {"label": "Time Out No.1", "full": "Time Out's 'best sandwiches in the world' (2025): No. 1, ahead of Epping Forest Oyster Shack and Kush Hialeah. David Lebovitz: 'the best sandwich in Paris.'", "cosign": "Time Out (2025); AFP (Apr 2026)"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "dishes": [{"name": "Jambon-beurre", "note": "Baguette, butter, ham off the bone, made to order; EUR 6.50 (AFP, Apr 2026)", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Le Spécial", "note": "Dry-cured country ham, fresh goat cheese and olive oil, a little dearer", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Plat du jour", "note": "The bistro's daily dish for a sit-down lunch", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Call ahead: one listing gives Mon 8:30-15:30, Tue-Fri 8:30-02:00, Sat 8:30-17:00, closed Sun; AFP (Apr 2026) says sandwiches sell from morning to midnight", "price_range": "Jambon-beurre EUR 6.50 (AFP, Apr 2026; seen Oct 2026)", "phone": "+33 1 42 61 05 88 (listing; not checked against own site)"},
    {"id": "v-breizh-cafe", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 39, "badge": "BORDIER BUTTER", "name": "Breizh Café", "short": "Breizh Café", "neighborhood": "3rd · Marais", "tags": ["3rd · Marais", "Confirmed trading 16 Oct 2026"], "productTags": ["galette", "crêpe", "cider"], "why": "Bertrand Larcher opened his first Breizh Café in Tokyo in the mid-1990s, then one in Cancale, and in 2007, per Mes Prestiges, this one on rue Vieille du Temple. The Japanese years show in the sourcing: Jean-Yves Bordier butter, IGP Breton buckwheat, Cancale oysters, and a cider list read like a wine card. The wood-panelled room serves all day, and Frommer's warns it fills up, so book.", "address": "109 rue Vieille du Temple, 75003 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Breizh+Caf%C3%A9+Paris", "category": "onedish", "verdict": "The best-sourced galette in Paris, and priced like it. Falsifiable: it works with Jean-Yves Bordier butter and IGP Breton buckwheat, and unlike Josselin it takes reservations — which you'll want.", "signature": "The Complète made with Bordier butter and organic buckwheat, or a galette with Label Rouge smoked salmon; finish with the salted-caramel crêpe and a regional cider flight.", "person": "Bertrand Larcher (founder)", "caveat": "price/booking — pricier than a classic crêperie; reserve ahead.", "hook": "Bertrand Larcher's Marais crêperie: Bordier butter, Breton buckwheat and a long cider list", "signal_chip": {"label": "Bordier butter", "full": "Cooks with Jean-Yves Bordier butter and IGP Breton buckwheat, with Cancale oysters and a long cider list; a Paris by Mouth favourite (old record, not re-checked).", "cosign": "Paris by Mouth"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "dishes": [{"name": "Galette complète", "note": "Ham, egg and cheese on organic buckwheat, cooked in Bordier butter", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Galette with Label Rouge smoked salmon", "note": "The fish galette", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Crêpe au caramel beurre salé", "note": "Salted-caramel crêpe to finish", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Cancale oysters", "note": "Shipped from Larcher's home coast", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Cider flight", "note": "Regional ciders from the long list", "kind": "drink"}], "hours": "Call ahead: Mes Prestiges (May 2026) gives daily 11:30-23:00; Frommer's gives Wed-Sat 11:30-23:00 and Sun 11:30-22:00", "reservation": "Reserve ahead", "hot_this_month": "Oysters are back: the house serves Cancale oysters with its buckwheat galettes and cider, a good October lunch in the Marais (checked 16 Oct 2026)."},
    {"id": "v-la-creperie-de-josselin", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 37, "badge": "TIME OUT 3/5", "name": "La Crêperie de Josselin", "short": "La Crêperie de Josselin", "neighborhood": "14th · Montparnasse", "tags": ["14th · Montparnasse", "Not confirmed for 2026 — call ahead"], "productTags": ["galette", "crêpe", "cider"], "why": "Gare Montparnasse was the Paris end of the line from Brittany, and Bretons who came for work settled around it; rue du Montparnasse still has a crêperie every few doors. Josselin, at number 67, is the one Time Out singles out for its queue, even on weekdays, and generous fillings at rarely more than EUR 20 a head. Do not mix it up with Le Petit Josselin, a separate house at number 59.", "address": "67 rue du Montparnasse, 75014 Paris", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=La+Cr%C3%AAperie+de+Josselin+Paris", "category": "onedish", "verdict": "The most honest galette in the Breton quarter by Montparnasse: skip the flourishes and order the complète. No reservations, and the cider arrives in a ceramic bowl, not a glass; the listings disagree on its closing days, so check before you go.", "signature": "La complète (ham, egg, Gruyère) with a bolée of cidre brut, then a salted-butter caramel crêpe to finish.", "person": "", "caveat": "No reservations, tight room, queue even on weekdays (Time Out). Closing days conflict across sources; check before crossing town. Time Out finds the crêpes generous but a little greasy.", "hook": "A rue du Montparnasse crêperie for lace-edged buckwheat and cider served in a bowl", "signal_chip": {"label": "Time Out 3/5", "full": "Time Out Paris 3/5: a queue even on weekdays, generous if slightly greasy crêpes, a bill rarely above EUR 20.", "cosign": "Time Out Paris"}, "status": "unverified", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "dishes": [{"name": "Galette complète", "note": "Ham, egg and Gruyère on buckwheat", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Bolée de cidre brut", "note": "Dry cider in a ceramic bowl", "kind": "drink"}, {"name": "Crêpe caramel beurre salé", "note": "Salted-butter caramel crêpe to finish", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Call ahead: Time Out gives Tue-Fri 11:15-15:15 and 18:00-23:30, Sat to midnight, Sun to 23:00, closed August and first week of January; another guide gives Tue-Sun 11:30-23:00; Lesrestos says daily; the old record says closed Mon and Tue", "price_range": "Rarely over EUR 20 a head (Time Out, undated; seen Oct 2026)"},
    {"id": "v-le-relais-de-l-entrecote", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 38, "badge": "STEAK-FRITES ONLY", "name": "Le Relais de l'Entrecôte", "short": "Le Relais de l'Entrecôte", "neighborhood": "6th · Saint-Germain", "tags": ["6th · Saint-Germain", "Not confirmed for 2026 — call ahead"], "productTags": ["steak-frites", "secret-sauce"], "why": "Paul Gineste de Saurs opened the original in 1959, and two houses now descend from it: the Relais de Venise near Porte Maillot, and this one, Marie-Paule Burrus's Relais de l'Entrecôte, with rooms on rue Saint-Benoît, rue Marbeuf and boulevard du Montparnasse and branches abroad, Dubai among them. The only question asked is how you want the meat cooked. The herb-butter sauce has never been published, and the steak arrives in two servings so the second is still hot.", "address": "20 rue Saint-Benoît, 75006 Paris (also 15 rue Marbeuf 8e, 101 bd du Montparnasse 14e)", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Le+Relais+de+l%27Entrec%C3%B4te+Paris", "category": "onedish", "verdict": "Not actually quick: a queue, then a full sit-down, a second helping of steak and frites included. No reservations, roughly EUR 30, and three Paris rooms (Saint-Benoît, Marbeuf, Montparnasse); the Marbeuf room was closed for works in 2026 and its reopening is unconfirmed, so this guide sends you to rue Saint-Benoît.", "signature": "There is no choice: a walnut-and-lettuce salad, then contrefilet steak-frites in the guarded herb-butter sauce, second serving included, about €30.", "person": "Marie-Paule Burrus (founder of the Relais de l'Entrecôte group)", "caveat": "Not quick: a queue, then a full sit-down. No bookings. The Marbeuf branch's reopening (old record: about 28 July 2026) was not confirmed; check your branch. Not the same house as the Relais de Venise.", "hook": "Steak-frites without a menu: walnut salad, contrefilet, a secret sauce, then a second helping", "signal_chip": {"label": "One set menu", "full": "One fixed menu since 1959: salad, then steak-frites in the guarded sauce, second helping included. Listed by Opinionated About Dining 2026 (casual), per a Pearl extract.", "cosign": "Opinionated About Dining 2026 (via Pearl)"}, "status": "unverified", "statusChecked": "2026-10-16", "dishes": [{"name": "Walnut and lettuce salad", "note": "The fixed starter", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Contrefilet steak-frites", "note": "With the house herb-butter sauce; second serving included", "kind": "dish"}], "price_range": "Call ahead: about EUR 30 in the old record; Frommer's (undated) gives a EUR 27 set menu (seen Oct 2026)", "reservation": "No reservations; queue"}
